WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
Speaker 1: Well, the last 24 hours have&nbsp;
been some of the most consequential that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:11.280
have had in months. We first heard from the&nbsp;
current attorney general, Merrick Garland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:19.280
that no one is above the law, that he is open to&nbsp;
prosecuting former President Donald Trump. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:25.760
then in addition to that, we learned, based on a&nbsp;
very interesting report from The Washington Post&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:32.240
that Donald Trump is currently under criminal&nbsp;
investigation by the Justice Department.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:38.080
This was the question, is the Justice Department&nbsp;
criminally investigating Donald Trump? Have they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:42.400
paid attention to the January six committee or to&nbsp;
what's going on in Georgia or to whatever else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.680
and done their own investigation and determined&nbsp;
this is worthy, not necessarily of charges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:52.000
but of criminal investigation. And the news&nbsp;
that broke over the last 24 hours is that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.800
answer is yes. We're going to look at both of&nbsp;
these things. I was going to lead with Garland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:04.000
but Garland being open to prosecuting Trump is&nbsp;
irrelevant if there's no probe. It now has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.400
revealed that there is indeed a probe. Washington&nbsp;
Post Report. Justice Department investigating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:16.400
Trump's actions in January six criminal probe.&nbsp;
People familiar with the probe said. Investigators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:22.560
are examining the former president's conversations&nbsp;
and have seized the phone records of top aides.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:30.160
Understand this is already historic. It is not&nbsp;
typical that a president leaves office and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.520
is under federal criminal investigation. Now it&nbsp;
may go to absolute nothing. This investigation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:42.720
may end up in the toilet, flushed 10 to 15 times.&nbsp;
But this is already an unprecedented and unique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:48.800
situation. The Justice Department is investigating&nbsp;
Trump's actions as part of its criminal probe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:54.640
of efforts to overturn the 2020 election,&nbsp;
according to four people familiar with the matter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:59.440
prosecutors who are questioning witnesses before&nbsp;
a grand jury, including two top aides to Vice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:04.560
President Pence, have asked in recent days&nbsp;
about conversations with Trump, his lawyers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:10.080
and others in his inner circle who sought to&nbsp;
substitute Trump allies for certified electors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:18.160
from some states. Joe Biden won. This is the&nbsp;
fake electoral scam. The prosecutors have asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:24.800
hours of detailed questions about meetings&nbsp;
Trump led in December of 2020 and January 2021.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:29.840
His pressure campaign on Pence to overturn the&nbsp;
election and what instructions Trump gave his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:35.280
lawyers and advisers about fake electors and&nbsp;
sending electors back to the states. The people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:40.000
said some of the questions focused directly on&nbsp;
Trump's on the extent of Trump's involvement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.880
in the fake elector effort led by his outside&nbsp;
lawyers, including John Eastman and Rudy Giuliani.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:51.520
This is major focus, and it appears that a lot&nbsp;
of the focus is on the fake elector scheme.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:59.120
The belief among some legal experts is that that's&nbsp;
probably the easiest one to prove, but it is all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:04.160
very much related generally to Trump's attempt to&nbsp;
overthrow the government and to install himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:09.520
The guy who did not win the illegitimate wouldn't&nbsp;
even be to say an illegitimate president. He just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:14.800
wouldn't. He wasn't the president. He&nbsp;
didn't win. The article goes on to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:20.320
Justice Department investigators in April received&nbsp;
phone records of key officials and aides in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:25.040
Trump administration, including Mark Meadows, his&nbsp;
chief of staff. That effort is another indicator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:31.600
of how expansive the January six probe had become&nbsp;
well before the hearings in June and July. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:37.280
is another really important question or answer.&nbsp;
I guess we would better say. We were wondering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:45.520
not only is was there a probe, but did the probe&nbsp;
only start because of the January six committee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:49.120
hearings? And it appears that the answer is no.&nbsp;
There was actually a criminal probe of Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:56.240
that started well before that. What do we know&nbsp;
about some of the build up to all of this? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:02.880
Jacqueline Alemany, who works for The Washington&nbsp;
Post and was part of breaking this story, appeared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:06.960
on MSNBC and talked about it. Listen to this.
Speaker 2: That's exactly right, Willie. We've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:14.000
reported that prosecutors who have recently&nbsp;
brought in people like Greg Jacobs and Marc Short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:20.080
and potentially more, we just you know, people&nbsp;
were brought up we might not be aware of just yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:24.880
But people who were very close to Mike Pence&nbsp;
had firsthand accounts and were witness to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:29.120
the pressure campaign being applied on him&nbsp;
to overturn the results of the election.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:34.160
Indeed, we're also in the room for other meetings&nbsp;
involving some of the constitutional schemes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:39.440
being put forth by Trump allies, people&nbsp;
like John Eastman. Before the president,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:44.320
they were asked about those interactions. They&nbsp;
were asked about meetings in December of 2021&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:51.680
and January of 2020 and December of 2020 and&nbsp;
January of 2021. We also know that they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:57.600
asked specifically about the pressure being&nbsp;
put on Mike Pence, according to sources.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:03.920
And we also know that these phone records that&nbsp;
have been newly reported and obtained by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:10.320
Justice Department included phone records from&nbsp;
Trump, top Trump aides and Mark Meadows as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:18.720
Someone else who was also in the room for a&nbsp;
lot of these really explosive and meetings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:23.280
that have recently garnered a lot of scrutiny.
Speaker 1: All right. So the point here that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:28.080
can only make a limited number of times in in&nbsp;
our program, but I think needs to be made, as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:35.440
we all have been to some degree, desensitized&nbsp;
by these last many years. And I include myself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:42.320
in that. But this is not normal. This is&nbsp;
extraordinary. These are extraordinary times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:47.120
that we are living in. The former president is&nbsp;
under criminal investigation by the following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:51.360
administrations. Justice Department. Now, what&nbsp;
could be the possible crimes? I've been reading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:57.840
up on this this morning as a non-lawyer who spoke&nbsp;
to some lawyers. There are three possibilities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:02.960
The easiest to prove would likely be conspiracy&nbsp;
to defraud the United States. This would be over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:10.000
the fake electors. Sending fake electors would be&nbsp;
committing a fraud, as Trump says, on the on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:14.800
United States. Other possible crimes. Obstruction&nbsp;
of an official proceeding. Now, there are lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:23.680
variations of this, but the one being looked&nbsp;
at by the January 6th committee seems to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:28.720
1512 C, which theoretically carries up to 20&nbsp;
years in prison. I don't expect that that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:33.200
ever going to be Trump's future, partially&nbsp;
because he won't live 20 years. And then also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:38.720
seditious conspiracy is possible. But again,&nbsp;
when we anything with the word sedition in it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:45.360
the bar to prove criminal intent is a difficult&nbsp;
one to overcome. And it's sort of related to why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:48.720
I know I've said this before, you don't often&nbsp;
see perjury charges, even though people do lie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:55.360
in court all the time. And we will just have to&nbsp;
follow it and see. Now, if you weren't sure about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.360 --> 00:07:00.960
the consequential nature of the midterm election&nbsp;
in 2022 and of course, the presidential in 2024,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:07.600
if you did think that those elections were&nbsp;
important before, they are absolutely above&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:14.160
and beyond important now. Trump got away to&nbsp;
different impeachments without conviction because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:23.040
Republicans had the Senate. You have to understand&nbsp;
that if control goes back to Republicans in 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:28.720
a lot of this stuff is completely dead in the&nbsp;
water now. Might charges be brought against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:33.680
Trump before that? Maybe. I don't think so. I&nbsp;
don't know. But I would expect Donald Trump to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:38.320
